<h2>Overview over the meta database</h2>
<h3 id="what-is-the-meta-database">What is the meta database?</h3>

<p>The meta database is a catalogue of known ${h.external_link('https://cldf.clld.org', label='CLDF')} datasets which have been
released on ${h.external_link('https://zenodo.org', label='Zenodo')}.</p>
<h3 id="methodology">Methodology</h3>

<p>In broad strokes the meta database is compiled thus:</p>
<ol>
<li>Search Zenodo for potential CLDF datasets</li>
<li>Download all of them</li>
<li>Open them up and compile some metadata about them</li>
<li>Dump the metadata into a CLDF dataset</li>
</ol>
<h4 id="finding-datasets">Finding datasets</h4>

<p>To find CLDF datasets the script employs a number of heuristics:</p>
<ul>
<li>The dataset must have a public release on Zenodo.</li>
<li>The dataset must contain a known CLDF-related keyword (see the variable
   <em>SEARCH_KEYWORDS</em> in <em>${h.external_link('https://github.com/cldf-datasets/cldf_meta/blob/master/cldf_meta_commands/updatemd.py', label='https://github.com/cldf-datasets/cldf_meta/blob/master/cldf_meta_commands/updatemd.py')}</em> for
   a full list), <em>OR</em></li>
<li>The dataset must be part of a community known to contain CLDF datasets (see
   the variable <em>SEARCH_COMMMUNITIES</em> in
   <em>${h.external_link('https://github.com/cldf-datasets/cldf_meta/blob/master/cldf_meta_commands/updatemd.py', label='https://github.com/cldf-datasets/cldf_meta/blob/master/cldf_meta_commands/updatemd.py')}</em> for a full list).</li>
<li>There are a bunch of manual rules for black- or whitelisting specific
   datasets.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="what-is-and-is-not-included">What is and is not included</h4>

<p>First of all, the meta database only recognises datasets using the <em>CLDF</em>
format.</p>
<p>Secondly, the scripts are only looking on Zenodo specifically (because that's
where we're depositing all our data).  Other data repositories are not supported
at the moment.</p>
<p>Also, there are some datasets that are explicitly excluded from the meta
database:</p>
<ul>
<li>The three reference catalogues <em>${h.external_link('https://glottolog.org/', label='Glottolog')}</em>,
   <em>${h.external_link('https://concepticon.clld.org/', label='Concepticon')}</em>, and <em>${h.external_link('https://clts.clld.org/', label='CLTS')}</em>.</li>
<li>The meta database itself (it is a CLDF dataset after all)</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="i-published-some-rad-data-but-its-not-in-here">I published some rad data but it's not in here!!!</h4>

<p>There could be a couple of reasons for this:</p>
<ul>
<li>If it's a recent publication I might just not have run my scripts in a while</li>
<li>My scripts might not have found the data while searching Zenodo</li>
<li>The data might be laid out in a fashion my scripts don't know about</li>
</ul>
<p>To fix this you could</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Edit your dataset on Zenodo and add <code>CLDF</code> to its keywords</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Send me a link to your dataset on Zenodo and I'll see if I can adapt my
   scripts accordingly (or just add the dataset manually)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="what-metadata-is-counted">What metadata is counted</h4>

<ul>
<li>Number of datasets</li>
<li>Which languages a dataset references (by glottocode)</li>
<li>How many languages, values, parameters, forms, examples, etc.</li>
<li>Also for each language: how many values, forms, examples, etc.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="terminology">Terminology</h3>

<p>Even in early stages of the project, people have voiced confusion about the
different terms used by the meta database.  This is entirely understandable;
we're dealing with a dataset about datasets, so terminology gets fuzzy fairly
quickly.  When someone says</p>
<ul>
<li>‘There are three datasets in the cldf/ folder,’</li>
<li>‘I uploaded the dataset to Zenodo,’ or</li>
<li>‘I analysed version 1.2 of the dataset,’</li>
</ul>
<p>their use of the word <em>dataset</em> invokes three separate technical concepts.  To
make matters worse, Zenodo's archives host all sorts of <em>stuff</em> (databases,
articles, media files, etc.) from a vast variety of scientific fields.  This
means that any attempt at creating one-size-fits-all terminology inevitably
results in terms so broad and abstract they become almost entirely meaningless
in isolation.</p>
<p>This section attempts to clear up the terminology used in the meta database.
For the most part the terminology is directly taken from Zenodo and mirrors the
way its archive is structured.</p>
<h4 id="zenodo-records">Zenodo records</h4>

<p>On Zenodo a <em>record</em> is the term for a specific upload.  Whenever a new dataset
is released a new record is created.  This includes the initial release as well
as every updated version of a dataset.  Every record gets its own DOI, which
makes it possible to cite/link to a specific versions of any given data set.</p>
<p>As an example: when Lexibank v1.0 was released, it got a record with the DOI
${h.external_link('https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7836668', label='10.5281/zenodo.7836668')}.  Then, when v2.0 came out, it got a new
record with the DOI ${h.external_link('https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14800315', label='10.5281/zenodo.14800315')}.</p>
<h4 id="zenodo-concepts">Zenodo concepts</h4>

<p>Sometimes it makes sense to refer to a dataset in general rather than a specific
version of it.  For this purpose, Zenodo introduces the idea of a <em>concept</em>.
A concept is basically just a DOI that refers to ‘all versions’ of a dataset at
once.  In practice, resolving a DOI will redirect you to whatever the latest
version of the dataset happens to be.</p>
<p>For example, if one wants to cite the Lexibank data as a whole, without any
specific version in mind, one can refer to the DOI
${h.external_link('https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5227817', label='10.5281/zenodo.5227817')}, which, at the time of writing, points
to Lexibank v2.2.</p>
<h4 id="cldf-datasets">CLDF Datasets</h4>

<p>While a record was referred to as ‘an upload’ above for simplicity, the reality
is that a record can contain <em>multiple files</em> – either directly or in the form
of archive formats (e.g. zip or tarballs).  On one hand this enables CLDF's
multi-file nature, on the other this allows for one record to contain <em>multiple</em>
CLDF datasets.</p>
<p>For example the record for ${h.external_link('https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14800315', label='Lexibank v2.0')} contains a single zip file
that harbours four separate CLDF datasets, identified by their metadata files:</p>
<ol>
<li><em>cldf/wordlist-metadata.json</em> (a wordlist)</li>
<li><em>cldf/lexicon-metadata.json</em> (a structure dataset)</li>
<li><em>cldf/phonemes-metadata.json</em> (a structure dataset)</li>
<li><em>cldf/phonology-metadata.json</em> (a structure dataset)</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="how-to-cite-the-project">How to cite the project</h3>

<p>Eventually this project will be released on Zenodo and get properly versioned
DOIs. Until then the following BibTeX snippet can serve as a possible interim
citation:</p>
<pre><code>@misc{cldf_meta,
  author = {Johannes Englisch},
  year = {forthcoming},
  title = {{CLDF-Meta}: A catalogue of {CLDF} databases},
  note = {Available online at https://meta.clld.org and https://github.com/cldf-datasets/cldf\_meta},
  url = {https://meta.clld.org},
}
</code></pre>

